Every character of s has to be sorted into digits, letters, spaces or other, and the function returns a dict that always contains all four keys with their counts, all zeros for an empty string.

The submission classifies with a helper and fills in the fixed set of keys at the end:

            if ch.isdigit():
                return 'digits'
            if ch.isalpha():
                return 'letters'
            if ch.isspace():
                return 'spaces'
            return 'other'
        return {k: counts.get(k, 0) for k in ('digits', 'letters', 'spaces', 'other')}

The helper tests each character against the three named categories and sends everything else to 'other', so every character lands in exactly one bucket. A Counter tallies the categories, and the final comprehension builds the output from the fixed tuple of four names, using get with a default of 0 so that missing categories still appear. That is what guarantees all four keys, including for the empty string.

For an input outside the examples I used 'a1 !?', which puts one character in each named category and two in the catch-all. The letter goes to letters, the 1 to digits, the space to spaces, and the exclamation and question marks to other, giving {'digits': 1, 'letters': 1, 'spaces': 1, 'other': 2}, which is right. A tab character also counts under spaces, which is a reasonable reading of the category. The approach relies only on per-character tests and never on the example strings, so it generalises to any text, including strings with no characters in some categories.

